26	// vizDefaultLimit caps how many symbols load into the graph. Large brains hold
27	// many thousands of symbols; the cap keeps the force simulation smooth and a
28	// "truncated" flag tells the UI to surface it. 0 = no cap.
29	const vizDefaultLimit = 4000
30	
31	// vizGraphMaxView is a safety ceiling on rendered nodes — NOT a feature cap. It's
32	// set well above any real brain's largest feature (this repo's history is ~172k)
33	// so the node-count slider reaches every feature's true total; it exists only to
34	// stop a pathological ?limit=99999999 request from trying to allocate the world.
35	const vizGraphMaxView = 300000
36	
37	// vizGraphMaxEdges is the absolute ceiling on rendered edges. The per-request cap
38	// (see handleGraph) scales with the node count so a full graph shows its real
39	// structure instead of an artificially sparse thread; this just bounds the worst
40	// case for payload + per-frame draw cost.
41	const vizGraphMaxEdges = 90000
42	
43	// vizGraphViewCap bounds how many symbols actually render in the semantic view.
44	// We load a wider pool (vizDefaultLimit), compute degree, then keep the most-
45	// connected symbols so the default graph is a legible, connected constellation
46	// instead of a hairball of arbitrary, mostly-isolated nodes. Drilling in via the
47	// inspector's "Expand neighbors" pulls in the rest on demand. 0 = render all.
48	const vizGraphViewCap = 1400
